<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ethiopia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ethiopia is a country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a> with one of the longest continuous state traditions in the world. It was the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a> and its successor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, fought a long war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>, and defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Adwa</a> in 1896 before being occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a> from 1936 to 1941.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ethiopia.jpg" alt="Ethiopia" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Drawn by SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ethiopia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ethiopia is a landlocked country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a>, covering 1,104,300 square kilometres. Within it is a vast highland complex of mountains and dissected plateaus, divided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-african-rift">Great Rift Valley</a> and surrounded by lowlands, steppes or semi-desert. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian Highlands</a> are the largest continuous mountain ranges in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tana">Lake Tana</a> in the north is the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-nile">Blue Nile</a>.</p><p>The terrain ranges from deserts along the eastern border to tropical forests in the south and extensive Afromontane areas in the north and southwest. The country includes Dallol, described as the world's hottest settlement. Endemic species include the gelada, the walia ibex and the Ethiopian wolf. Ethiopia has the second-largest number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ethiopia is regarded as one of the earliest sites of anatomically modern humans. The Omo remains, excavated in the southwest, are dated to around 200,000 years ago, and Omo-Kibish I is the oldest anatomically modern Homo sapiens skeleton currently known. The hominid Lucy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australopithecus">Australopithecus</a> afarensis, found in the Awash Valley in 1974, is estimated to have lived 3.2 million years ago. A 30,000-year-old rock shelter at Fincha Habera in the Bale Mountains, at 3,469 metres, is the earliest known permanent human occupation at high altitude.</p><p>The Kingdom of D'mt extended over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> and northern Ethiopia from about 980 BCE, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Kingdom of Aksum</a> maintained a unified civilisation for 900 years; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> arrived there in 330 CE. After Aksum collapsed in 960, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe dynasty</a> ruled the north-central region until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yekuno-amlak">Yekuno Amlak</a> overthrew it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ansata">Battle of Ansata</a> in 1270, inaugurating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomonic-dynasty">Solomonic dynasty</a>. Under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amda-seyon-i">Amda Seyon I</a> (r. 1314 to 1344) the empire expanded against neighbouring Muslim territories, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-adal-war">Ethiopian-Adal War</a> (1529 to 1543) weakened it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>, Ethiopia defeated an Italian invasion at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a> in 1896 and remained independent during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa">Scramble for Africa</a>. Italy invaded in 1936, and the country was liberated by the British Army in 1941; full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> was restored in 1944. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a>, took power in 1974 after deposing Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> and ruled for nearly 17 years. Ethiopia is now a federal parliamentary republic.</p><p>It is a multi-ethnic state with more than 80 ethnic groups, of which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oromo">Oromo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> and Tigrayans are the four largest. The Ethiopian Orthodox Tewahedo Church is the largest denomination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, the most widely professed faith. Its capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, hosts the headquarters of the African Union Commission.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ethiopia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ethiopian-Adal War drew in both global rivals of the age: about 400 Portuguese musketeers fought for <b>Ethiopia</b>, while Ottoman musketeers fought for Adal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Its centres are traditionally placed at Mareeg in central Somalia and at Qalaafo on the upper Shabelle, in what is now <b>Ethiopia</b>’s Somali Region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aksumite Empire arose in the highlands of northern <b>Ethiopia</b> and Eritrea around the first century CE, though its roots go back to the pre-Aksumite civilisation that had absorbed South Arabian cultural and linguistic influences centuries earlier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Succession was matrilineal, passing to the son of the king's sister rather than to his own son, and Abu al-Makarim may describe a mobile royal encampment of the kind later known in the Funj sultanate, <b>Ethiopia</b> and Darfur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ethiopia</b> became the Soviet bloc's closest ally in Africa and one of the best-armed states of the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ethiopia</b>'s survival in 1543 owed much to some 400 Portuguese musketeers, whose firepower helped Emperor Galawdewos defeat and kill Ahmad Gran at Wayna Daga.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
